we've been talking for years on the  program about how to best get health  care to everyone and I'm using that  phrasing as opposed to saying Medicare  for all or nationalized health care or  whatever else because there are actually  different ways of getting health care to  everyone and many different countries at  our level of development have different  systems all of which relatively  effectively achieve that goal and this  is something that's really important to  be informed about because the right  likes to give these out of context  anecdotes and use general talking points  to try to sort of meld all of the health  care systems in different countries  together and derail the conversation  I'll give you some examples and then  you'll understand what I mean we hear Oh  healthcare for all is bad because people  wait forever for care in Canada or  healthcare for all is bad because the  UK's public hospitals are terrible or  whatever and the point that I want to  make to you today is that there is no  one health care for all is bad talking  point that works because these countries  all have different systems when in the  United States we say the rest of the  developed world has figured this out  that's true  but in different ways and we've  mentioned before the difference between  for example single-payer systems and  nationalized health care those are two  different things the whole point here is  everybody has access to care quality  care at a price that they can afford  sometimes the price being zero at the  tail-end but paid for through taxation  but the systems are very very different  now I am relatively agnostic as to how  we get there  what I care about is what is the system  cost in total and is it affordable for  the end users what does it do in terms  of access and what is the quality of  health care that is provided and wait  times would be part of what I would  consider quality so let's talk about  some of the differ  is that that are sort of out there  Canada and the UK  both have single-payer systems but in  Canada as we've recently talked about  because of a procedure Rand Paul is  going to be undergoing in Canada in  Canada the providers are still  overwhelmingly private although they  often receive money from provincial  governments the insurance is  single-payer in Canada at the province  level but the health care delivery  system has not been socialized and taken  over by the government Canadians will  often pay for additional coverage  through jobs for prescriptions dentistry  optometry the government ends up  spending about 70% of all health care  expenditures then we look at a country  like Britain we look at the UK health  care itself has been socialized or  nationalized you can say meaning that  the government is providing most of the  care through the national health  services there is a private system in  the UK only about 10% of people have it  but the government does about 80% of the  healthcare spending but again the  government is providing a lot of the  care through hospitals and clinics that  they run oh we've talked before about  the Singapore approach which is very  different actually did an interview  about the Singapore health care system  in Singapore there is cheap and  sometimes free care made available in  government-run hospitals  workers contribute about 37 percent of  their wages into mandated savings  accounts those accounts aren't just used  for health care they can also be used  for a housing for insurance for  investments for education and employers  also contribute a piece to it so it is a  sort of taxation plus government-run  model that is still more market driven  than some of the others you have the  opportunity to pay more for private  rooms or more luxurious care if you can  afford it or if that's important for you  since so much runs through the  government in Singapore the government  will go  she ate drug prices they will be  involved in setting doctor salaries etc  it's way cheaper than the United States  four point nine percent of GDP versus  17% of GDP in the United States it's  also a much more efficient system but it  does have some shortcomings which we can  dig into a little bit later let's look  at Australia Australia has free  inpatient care at public hospitals and  they also have a voluntary private  insurance program the government in  Australia is paying about 85% of  outpatient services the rest is made up  with this optional private insurance if  you look at France in France everybody  is buying health insurance that is sold  by nonprofits they don't have or it is a  non-profit healthcare industry it's  financed through taxes and it covers up  to 80% of healthcare costs then it's up  to you if you want to get insurance for  that additional 20 or 30% that's not  covered or pay for it out of pocket then  we look at a system like Switzerland  Switzerland has a universal health care  system where everybody has to buy  insurance from nonprofits 30% of buyers  get subsidies based on income as a  percentage of GDP it's a little bit more  expensive but they have extraordinarily  good health outcomes as well as access  to care in Switzerland so all of this is  just to get us to today's conversation  about Germany because Germany's health  care system is not discussed nearly as  often as it probably should be and over  the weekend there was an op-ed written  in The New York Times about the health  care system by Jamie DAW who's a  Columbia public health or health policy  professor and the professor professor da  says that the German system is better  Germany's system achieves universal  coverage for most services it costs less  than Canada's obviously it cost less  than the American system which is one of  the worst while simultaneously being one  of the most expensive  and it is not a single-payer system  importantly it's a multi payer system  and it has a really interesting history  and have spoken about some of this  before the system Germany has right now  goes back to Otto von Bismarck in 1883  and we actually spoke to former Cigna  executive wendell potter on the program  about this and he talks about it in his  book deadly spin the united states at  one point between World War one and  World War two was actually heading in  the direction of this Otto von Bismarck  German system but the timing of it was  such that the United States didn't want  to seem to be doing the thing that  Germans are doing because Germany was a  growing enemy of the United States the  system was abandoned the idea for that  system was abandoned in the United  States and we ended up with the system  we have now which is truly a disaster  the system Germany has you have to have  health insurance okay there are about a  hundred private nonprofits that are  insurers that you can choose from  workers and employers are sharing the  cost through payroll taxes similar to  what a medicare-for-all system might be  in the United States and the government  finances coverage for kids and  unemployed people now important detail  just because the cost is shared by  employers and employees doesn't mean  that the plans are tied to your employer  or to having employment and because the  system is funded through progressive  taxation access is actually based on  need not one's ability to pay or how  much you contribute is based on your  income rather than whether you're  healthy or you're not healthy and  there's a sort of genius element to this  which is that all of the taxes for these  programs are collected centrally and  then they're distributed to the  nonprofit insurers on the basis of the  health of the people that are members so  let me give you an example if you're a  non-profit insurer and the people you  are insuring tend to be more sick than  another non  ensure you will get more of that money  that is centrally collected and managed  by the German government in this central  fund in total they spend way less per  person than in the United States and in  many other countries that have other  forms of universal health care but  importantly they're not limiting access  to care that people need which is often  a talking point we hear about some of  these systems the right loves to say  care is rationed in a lot of these  countries number-one care is also  effectively rationed in the United  States in other ways and it's also just  not true that in these systems you don't  get the care that you need you get the  care you need there may be less urgent  procedures for which you might have to  wait a little longer than you  potentially would in the United States  but that doesn't mean people aren't  getting the care that they need and this  gets us back to the Bernie Sanders  proposal this is actually very similar  to what Bernie Sanders has proposed  which is using payroll taxes both on  employees and employers the difference  is that in Germany the money is going to  nonprofit insurers instead of a  single-payer system and not only in  Germany but also in Austria and the  Netherlands they have had serious  reduction to the cost of care down to  Medicare levels which is considered to  be something good to aspire to in terms  of cost of care levels that we have here  in the United States the other really  interesting element about the German  health care system is that it gets  everyone coverage it doesn't stifle  innovation it still removes profit from  health insurance which I don't believe  should be there as a social democrat and  it maintains choice which is what a lot  of people fear will be taken away under  some government-run systems so no system  is perfect they're basically all better  than what we have and it's really  important to understand that the talking  points that the right loves to use on  this simply don't apply in general  because every country has  different system in terms of Bernie  Sanders and Medicare for all my  suggestion would be we don't need to be  overly attached to Medicare for all  Medicare for all has some issues one is  that people who have Medicare in the  United States 80% have additional  coverage according to a recent study  they're either having Medicare plus some  kind of employer plan Medicare plus  Medigap insurance some people are poor  enough that they have Medicare plus  Medicaid and there's a few other things  in there so once we understand that  let's remove the sort of emotion from  the entire thing let's look at a system  like Germany's at the same time that we  consider Medicare for all type systems a  nationalized system like the UK has a  system that is more like Canada's all  paths forward should be considered a  once again I am in general agnostic as  long as there is a direct path to moving  to another system because every single  developed country has a better  functioning system at a lower cost than  the one that we have I want to hear from  you about this are you preferential to  the German system you prefer the British  system the Canadian system if you're  particularly attached to Medicare for  all let me know why I'm on Twitter at D  Pakman and the show is on Twitter at  David Pakman show

